WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.840
Film Courage: What is your favorite chapter
in the book [Jill’s book THE NUTSHELL TECHNIQUE:

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:02.840
Crack the Secret of Successful Screenwriting]?

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.580
One that you are especially proud of or a
topic that just really gets you going?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.640
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
One of my favorites is the ‘Flaw’ because

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.330
I think the flaw is so important [Chapter
9 in the book - Flaw].

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.830
I think it’s the longest chapter in the
book and I loved writing about it.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:22.661
It just is an area that…it’s the one tool
that I can give a new writer right off of

00:00:22.661 --> 00:00:25.529
the bat that will make a huge improvement.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:27.560
And a lot of writers don’t know anything
about it.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:35.810
It really is the most fundamental important
thing for story building to realize the importance

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:41.210
of having a really strong flaw and it’s
a rookie mistake sometimes I see that they

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.860
didn’t even know that there protagonist
was supposed to have a flaw, right?

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.950
We are not used to thinking about our heroes
as having flaws.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.710
They are not flawed!

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.140
Everyone else is the one that is messed up.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.780
But if everyone else is screwed up then you
are making your protagonist just a victim

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.559
of these things happening.

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:07.880
It’s a deeper level to think about what
is the flaw in them that is actually making

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.560
their problem worse.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.299
That is one of my favorite things to talk
about with writers, to explore.

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:20.539
It’s just one of the most fundamental things
to understanding who your character is is

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:22.700
that one single thing - the flaw.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:28.539
So it’s the longest chapter I try to explore
it the most and I think it’s one of the

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:35.710
things that will give writers the most yield
as far as understanding story and characters

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.340
to really give some thought to what is my
protagonist central flaw.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.819
Film Courage: Are there certain movies you
recommend that they watch which show an especially

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:44.819
flawed character?

00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:47.329
Jill: Oooh…I love movies with flawed characters!

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:50.229
Film Courage: LEAVING LAS VEGAS?

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:58.740
Jill: Hmmmm…I haven’t Nutshelled that
movie but I would argue that it would be a

00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.740
tragedy.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:06.529
I would argue that it wouldn’t have a satisfying
enough second act upturn.

00:02:06.529 --> 00:02:09.679
But I’d have to go back and look at it to
see.

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:18.049
I like movies that are more technically comedies
so structurally they have a happy ending but

00:02:18.049 --> 00:02:26.140
they have really flawed characters because
it’s really easy and not very interesting

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.739
to have likable characters.

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:34.980
But how much more delicious is it to have
a really unlikable character and then by the

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.569
end you have us cheering for them.

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:41.290
That’s really good writing.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.069
So I’ll point out one movie that I love
is the movie THE VERDICT.

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:54.129
It’s a Paul Newman movie and this guy, he’s
a lawyer (you’ve heard of ambulance chasers?),

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:56.859
this guy is a hearse chaser.

00:02:56.859 --> 00:03:05.860
He crashes funerals under the presents of
having known the deceased and he just finds

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.860
them in obituaries.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.800
He preys on these unsuspecting widows trying
to get work.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:20.500
He’s such a hardcore alcoholic that he can’t
pick up his morning shot with his donut without

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.500
the shakes.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.209
He’s got to lean over at one point to drink
that.

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:28.040
At one point he punches a woman in the face.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:34.489
The character is unbelievably unlikeable but
by the end of the movie we are rooting for

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:35.489
this guy.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:39.109
That is amazing screenwriting, that’s amazing
screenwriting, right?

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:42.099
If you can get us to care about this guy.

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:44.590
And we do, we really care.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:49.480
That is the depth of how great the screenwriting
is and how great the performance is (it’s

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.780
one of Paul Newman’s greatest performances).

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.629
So I love unlikable characters that you can
get us to like by the end.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:00.340
That tells you something.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.500
So really flawed characters like that.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:11.150
Another interesting movie to me (a more recent
example) is FLIGHT.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:17.230
For me what was really interesting…so we
have another alcoholic character (they make

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:18.350
good characters), nicely flawed characters.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.519
They are great.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.280
And by the way, alcoholism is not a flaw for
our purposes.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.330
You want to find what is the underlying flaw.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.380
So it’s going to be different for different
alcoholics.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:35.620
In THE VERDICT he has no sense of self worth.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.990
In FLIGHT the alcoholic has this incredible
sense of entitlement.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.389
So it’s going to be different.

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:43.780
You want to dig deeper.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:46.420
What is the flaw underneath it?

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:52.130
And what I love about FLIGHT is has the most…it’s
a wonderful example of the most surprising

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:56.300
yet incredibly satisfying third act.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.330
I don’t want to give spoilers if someone
hasn’t seen it.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:09.650
But all just say that the character the protagonist
is in…we want him to get off scott free

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:14.650
because we are rooting for this guy even though
he’s an alcoholic and he’s got a lot of

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:19.099
issues and we kind of now he shouldn’t go
free.

00:05:19.099 --> 00:05:27.919
And things don’t go the nest possible way
but it’s incredibly surprising and satisfying

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:35.910
and so it has a really unusual (particularly
for a Hollywood movie) a really unusual third

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:43.970
act and ending where we have a character under
outward circumstances you wouldn’t say on

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:48.139
paper that sounded like a happy ending and
yet he’s the happiest we’ve seen him the

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:51.210
whole movie even though things didn’t go
his way.

